                    briefing_text_r3:       `Turn the dinner-table habit (~COP$6.000 millones/year, an 88% payout) into a <strong>formal dividend policy</strong> that funds growth. Then solve the abroad-based sibling who lives on that income and threatens to <em>block any cut</em> at the shareholders' meeting. A financially clean number the owners reject is not a solution — design a bridge. Quantify the cash you free for reinvestment, and prepare the argument: retaining now raises long-run family wealth.`,
                    briefing_title_r4:      `Round 4 — Reform governance & lock the deal`,
                    briefing_text_r4:       `The retailer's procurement and the bank's credit committee both ask: <em>“Who governs this company?”</em> Design the governance reform that makes Lácteos bankable and scalable — independent board seats with a real audit/finance remit, genuine separation of ownership and management, and a founder transition from operator-with-veto to <strong>chair-with-defined-authority</strong>. Then pitch financing + dividend + governance as one coherent package. Cosmetic reform — or keeping Don Aníbal's informal veto under the new chart — loses the best pricing and the signature.`,

