                    coach_default:          `Decision logged.`,
                    coach_r1_cashgap:       `<strong>That's the right read.</strong> A profitable firm can still be cash-constrained: the COP$22.000M build plus ≈COP$6.200M trapped by 75-day terms swamp your COP$5.500M of cash when 88% of profit leaves as a dividend. Profit is not cash — and you saw it on the balance sheet, not the income statement.`,
                    coach_r1_income:        `<strong>Income-statement myopia.</strong> You took the COP$40.000M contract on the revenue case. Now Month 5: COP$22.000M spent on the plant, the chain pays on day 75, receivables ballooned, and your cash account shows COP$5.500M. The deal is profitable and you're insolvent. Where did the cash go — and where did you model it?`,
                    coach_r1_profit:        `<strong>This isn't a margin problem.</strong> 13% EBITDA is normal for a LATAM dairy SME. The firm is profitable; it's cash-poor because of the working-capital trap and the dividend, not because of profitability. Diagnose the liquidity gap, not the margin.`,
                    coach_r1_growth:        `<strong>Growth does not self-fund here.</strong> The 42% revenue jump <em>worsens</em> liquidity first — 75-day terms trap cash before any extra profit arrives. Betting that revenue covers a COP$28.200M cash need is how a profitable firm goes insolvent.`,
                    coach_r1_sharp:         `<strong>Sharp diagnosis.</strong> You named the cash-constraint AND sized the gap at ≈COP$28.200M — CapEx plus the working-capital trap. That's the balance-sheet read that lets you design real financing instead of celebrating a revenue number.`,
                    coach_r1_capexonly:     `<strong>You sized the CapEx but missed the trap.</strong> COP$22.000M builds the plant, but 75-day terms lock ≈COP$6.200M more in receivables. Fund only the CapEx and you're still cash-short the moment the contract ramps. The requirement is ≈COP$28.200M.`,
                    coach_r1_lowhonesty:    `<strong>Governance is not a soft detail here.</strong> Downplaying the absent board and the founder's veto means the bank prices you as higher-risk regardless of your ratios. Name governance as a financial variable now, or pay for it later.`,
                    coach_r2_breach:        `<strong>You breached the covenant.</strong> Funding mostly with debt to protect the dividend pushes leverage past the 3,0× ceiling — one bad quarter in the ramp and you're in technical default. Was preserving an 88% payout worth handing the company to the bank?`,
                    coach_r2_thin:          `<strong>Razor-thin headroom.</strong> You're sitting just under 3,0×. If EBITDA dips during the ramp you breach. The bank sees the risk and prices it. Trim the debt tranche or add a cheaper lever.`,
                    coach_r2_underfund:     `<strong>The plan doesn't fund the gap.</strong> Your mix sums short of ≈COP$28.200M — you'd accept the contract and run out of cash mid-build. Fund the full requirement before you commit to the retailer.`,
                    coach_r2_dilution:      `<strong>Reflexive dilution.</strong> You sold a big stake to an outside fund before exhausting leasing, a phased dividend cut and a sized debt tranche — the founder is going pale across the table. Dilution is the right answer last, not first.`,
                    coach_r2_overequity:    `<strong>That's a lot of equity.</strong> Outside money strengthens the balance sheet but you handed away more of a 35-year family business than this gap required. Size the equity to what the cheaper levers can't cover.`,
                    coach_r2_balanced:      `<strong>A disciplined, covenant-safe mix.</strong> A sized debt tranche, leasing for the plant, a measured dividend cut and only the equity you actually need — leverage stays under 3,0× and the balance sheet strengthens. That's the pecking order done right.`,
                    coach_r3_target:        `<strong>A formal target payout is exactly the move.</strong> A reduced ≈35% payout with staged restoration tied to milestones funds growth, separates company cash needs from owner income, and gives the family a credible path back. That's policy, not a dinner-table habit.`,
                    coach_r3_residual:      `<strong>A residual policy funds growth first.</strong> Distributing only what's left after reinvestment is disciplined, but it gives the family no predictability — pair it with a clear restoration path or trust frays.`,
                    coach_r3_suspend:       `<strong>A full suspension self-funds — but it's blunt.</strong> Zero payout during the ramp with no transition starves the household income the dependent sibling relies on. Stage it, or you'll lose the vote.`,
                    coach_r3_keep:          `<strong>Keeping the 88% payout starves the growth.</strong> If the family takes COP$6.000M out every year, retained earnings fund almost nothing and you lean entirely on debt and dilution. High payout has a financing cost — and here it's the contract.`,
                    coach_r3_bridgeright:   `<strong>That's family-firm finance done right.</strong> A formal target policy AND a bridge for the dependent sibling means the reform funds growth and survives the shareholders' meeting. You negotiated the cut within ownership instead of imposing it.`,
                    coach_r3_buyout:        `<strong>A full premium buyout mid-squeeze is expensive.</strong> You solved the veto by spending scarce cash you need for the plant — clean politically, costly financially. A bridge loan and staged buyback cover their income without draining the build.`,
                    coach_r3_none:          `<strong>You imposed the cut.</strong> Financially clean, but the sibling abroad lives on that income and shows up to block the reform — and they have the votes to stall past your 45-day deadline. A correct number the owners reject is not a solution. What bridge did you offer?`,
                    coach_r3_ignore:        `<strong>Ignoring the dependent shareholder is a bet you lose.</strong> They show up to the meeting, block the cut, and you miss the deadline. Family-firm finance is negotiated within ownership, not voted around it.`,
                    coach_r4_independent:   `<strong>Independent seats with a real audit/finance remit answer the question.</strong> When the credit committee asks 'who governs this company?', a board with genuine power and outside directors is the credible answer that unlocks the bank's pricing and the retailer's signature.`,
                    coach_r4_advisory:      `<strong>An advisory committee is a half-step.</strong> Advice with no binding power doesn't de-risk the bank — they want decision rights and oversight, not a sounding board. Give the board real authority.`,
                    coach_r4_onefriend:     `<strong>A family friend with no remit isn't independence.</strong> The bank sees through a single hand-picked seat with no formal mandate. Independence and an audit/finance remit are what change the risk view.`,
                    coach_r4_paper:         `<strong>Cosmetic governance.</strong> You added 'recruit an independent director' as a footnote — but the best pricing and the signature are <em>conditional</em> on credible governance. It isn't compliance here; it's the price of the capital and the contract.`,
                    coach_r4_definedboard:  `<strong>Real separation of ownership and management.</strong> An independent board AND a founder moved to chair-with-defined-authority is the structure outsiders can bank on — defined decision rights, not a permanent override.`,
                    coach_r4_figurehead:    `<strong>Sidelining the founder at a stroke breaks the family.</strong> An honorary chair with no functions answers the bank but humiliates a 71-year-old founder mid-deal — and the family fractures. Separation means defined authority, not abrupt removal.`,
                    coach_r4_veto:          `<strong>You drew professional governance over a preserved bottleneck.</strong> A board, independent seats, an audit committee — and a line that says 'major spending requires Don Aníbal's approval.' Separation means defined authority, not a permanent veto. What does the chair actually decide now?`,
                    coach_r4_integrated:    `<strong>One coherent package.</strong> Financing, dividend and governance presented as a single decision is exactly what de-risks the bank and the retailer — the funding is credible because the governance and the policy back it. That's how you lock the deal.`,
                    coach_r4_financeonly:   `<strong>Just the numbers won't close it.</strong> Treating governance as a separate formality is precisely the gap the credit committee is probing. The pricing is conditional on governance — present them as one decision.`,
                    coach_r4_prestige:      `<strong>Prestige doesn't price risk.</strong> The retailer and bank don't sign on the family brand — they sign on credible governance and a funded, covenant-safe plan. Lead with the structure, not the reputation.`,

